invariant: INV-16
file: src/rsa_sign.c
catches: test/lint-wide-multiply.sh
reason: the ladder starts at the highest set bit of the blinded exponent
  instead of at its top bit position, so the number of Montgomery
  multiplications a signature runs is that exponent's bit length. Every
  signature is still correct, and every vector still passes, because the
  skipped bits are zeros that multiply by one -- the whole cost is a
  timing channel. The scan that finds that bit reads the exponent in a
  loop whose trip count is the secret, and the branch count rises on
  every clang spec
  (https://github.com/c4milo/chapulin/issues/141).

--- old
    for (size_t i = 32 * exponent_words; i-- > 0;) {
        uint32_t bit = (exponent[i >> 5] >> (i & 31)) & 1;
--- new
    size_t top = 32 * exponent_words;
    while (top > 0 && ((exponent[(top - 1) >> 5] >> ((top - 1) & 31)) & 1) == 0) {
        top--;
    }
    for (size_t i = top; i-- > 0;) {
        uint32_t bit = (exponent[i >> 5] >> (i & 31)) & 1;
